title: "MICA canon"
type: dataset
authors:
  - given-names: Frank
    family-names: Böhm
version: "0.1.0"
license: CC-BY-4.0
repository-code: "https://github.com/arcencilo-family/mica-canon"
date-released: "2026-11-16"
abstract: >-
  MICA is an approach to reading what an AI agent does. Its instruments are gates:
  each tests one property and returns a verdict. A reader may alarm, stay silent, or
  say that it cannot assess, and that third answer is treated as a result, not a
  failure. Under the gates sits a health substrate that sets how far any verdict may
  be trusted. Around them stand human roles, doctor and arbiter, where escalation ends.

  This canon is the design behind those instruments: 50 chapters on the substrate, the
  gates, the roles and the principles that hold them together. Each claim carries a
  tag saying whether it is load-bearing, grounded in observed runs, speculative, or
  still owed.

  The canon states the intended design. What runs today is shown by two separate code
  repositories, MICA machine and MICA bridge, published as an alpha with their known
  limits. Where canon and code differ, the code is what runs. The work is offered for
  others to read, test and develop; it is not a finished or certified system.
keywords:
  - artificial intelligence
  - AI safety
  - agents
  - monitoring
